rota lap 4191, row 1 -- Air, the row that feels: touch meets a thing where it ends, and the page's own test is to pull one part and see whether the others move with it. Pulled the number out of a fold shelf's headline; the filename, the title and the Rows header stayed put. That is a braid, and it was standing in this ship's own shelf.
think Last lap's card left a measurement unspent -- a folded REDS row spells its number in filename, Rows header and headline, and reds_spine_derive reads only the headline. The air row's test turned that reading into a census.
think Counted the spellings and found a FOURTH the elder note missed: the H1 title. Census over 291 tracked shelves with range semantics -- a filename names a span, never a set.
think Six candidates fell out of a naive reading and five were honest prose: a title naming %364 as its subject, two Rows headers reciting a renumber history after their declaration, an elder shelf whose span is wider than the rows it holds, and a bare prose mention of another row. Narrowed the reading to the three shapes that are declarations rather than mentions.
think One real disagreement remained, and it was mine: REDS-a-reader-that-walks-past-its-subject-rows-572.md carried `row %568` in its own H1 after the spine renumbered it %568 -> %566 -> %572 the lap before.
think Chose a separate guard rather than a fourth reading inside reds_spine_derive. That scan compares this tree's bindings against the anointed remote; this one compares a shelf against its own name. Two subjects, and single-stranded says one module about one thing.
think Chose the FILENAME as the authority rather than the headline, because the filename is the link target 291 living citations arrive at, where the other three spellings are labels a reader reads.
obs A guard that reports only what it gates reads as though it covered the room, so title_declaring=63 and rows_declaring=26 are printed beside shelves_read=291: the green says the shelves that DECLARE agree, rather than every shelf is right.
obs The control's welcomes cost as much thought as its refusals. Three honest shapes stand in this archive today and a looser reading calls every one of them wrong, so each is planted in the pen and each must pass.
obs The control's head comment graded C+ on its first reading -- 62% negative over 8 sentences, grade 12 against Door's 9. Molted in place at depth 1, leading with what the pen does rather than what it refuses: A (92).
obs The card had 4 bytes of slack, so the elder COPAL account folded to its own shelf and the new block was written to 858 bytes against the elder's 856.
obs The cold open read 158 guards, 1 red -- the recorded live_group_plant pen flake, which the card already carries as a concurrent-pen family and which Diffuser refuted inside its own lap.
obs The rebase proved the guard on its own row. `xy` had spent %574 on `20260907.144904` while this lap held it, so `reds_spine_derive` read `squatting %574 ... derives above %576` and the row renumbered to %577 -- moved in all four spellings at once (filename by `git mv`, H1, `Rows:` header, headline), plus the fold recital line, the card, the elder-account shelf and this log. `reds_shelf_name` then read `title_disagrees=0` over 295 shelves, which is the sweep proving itself.
obs Four ledger guards reddened on the hot pass and all four had one root: the local spine jumped 572 to 574 because 573 stood on `xy` and not yet in this checkout. The rebase closed three; `reds_ledger_headline_write.sh write` closed the fourth, and the pin now recites 577.
obs One pen number was over the anointed spine's `shared_max` -- `%999` in the control's prose-mention plant -- so `unshared_citation` bit a fixture. Moved to `%499`, a bound number, with the plant's meaning unchanged.
